var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"location":"elements/#elements","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"This page documents how Khepri BIM elements are realized in Revit. Each element maps to specific Revit API calls through the b_* backend operations.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Path-Handling","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Path Handling","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Many Revit elements (walls, slabs, roofs, curtain walls) are defined by paths. Revit can only handle lines and arcs — not splines. The locs_and_arcs function converts Khepri paths to Revit's point-and-angle representation:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Khepri Path Type Conversion\nOpenPolygonalPath Points with zero angles (straight segments)\nClosedPolygonalPath Points with zero angles (straight segments)\nArcPath Start and end points with the arc amplitude angle\nCircularPath Two antipodal points with two π angles (two semicircles)\nSplinePath Not supported — throws an error","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"For composite paths containing both line and arc segments, locs_and_arcs decomposes them into the point+angle format that Revit expects.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Levels","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Levels","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Levels are fundamental to Revit's BIM model. Every floor, wall, column, and stair references a level.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"# Create a level at elevation 3.0 meters\nmy_level = level(3.0)\n\n# Levels are realized on demand\nwall(xy(0, 0), xy(10, 0), bottom_level=my_level)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Levels are realized via FindOrCreateLevelAtElevation(elevation) — if a level already exists at that elevation, it is reused.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Horizontal-Elements","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Horizontal Elements","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/#Slabs-(Floors)","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Slabs (Floors)","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Slabs support three path types through multiple dispatch:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"# Polygonal slab\nslab(closed_polygonal_path([xy(0,0), xy(10,0), xy(10,8), xy(0,8)]))\n\n# Rectangular slab\nslab(rectangular_path(xy(0, 0), 10, 8))\n\n# Curved slab (arcs and lines)\nslab(some_closed_path_with_arcs)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Polygonal paths → CreatePolygonalFloor(vertices, level_id)\nRectangular paths → converted to vertices, then CreatePolygonalFloor\nOther closed paths → decomposed via locs_and_arcs into CreatePathFloor(points, angles, level_id)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Regions with holes: When a Region is passed (an outer path with inner paths), the outer path creates the slab, then each inner path creates an opening via CreatePolygonalOpening or CreatePathOpening.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Roofs","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Roofs","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"roof(closed_path, level=some_level)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Roofs are created via CreatePathRoof(points, angles, level_id, family_id). The path is always decomposed through locs_and_arcs.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Ceilings","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Ceilings","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"ceiling(closed_path, level=some_level)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Similar to slabs, ceilings support polygonal, rectangular, and general closed paths:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Polygonal → CreatePolygonalCeiling(vertices, level_id)\nRectangular → converted to vertices, then CreatePolygonalCeiling\nOther closed paths → CreatePathCeiling(points, angles, level_id)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Regions are also supported — the outer path creates the ceiling, then inner paths are ignored (only the outer boundary is used).","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Vertical-Elements","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Vertical Elements","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/#Walls","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Walls","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Walls are one of the most complex elements in Revit. KhepriRevit distinguishes between connected and unconnected walls:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"# Connected wall (spans between two levels)\nwall(xy(0, 0), xy(10, 0),\n     bottom_level=level(0),\n     top_level=level(3))\n\n# Unconnected wall (specified by height, no top level)\nwall(xy(0, 0), xy(10, 0),\n     bottom_level=level(0),\n     top_level=unconnected_level(3))","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Connected walls (top level has a valid ElementId): Created via CreateLineWall(vertices, base_level_id, top_level_id, family_id)\nUnconnected walls (top level id = -1): Created via CreateUnconnectedLineWall(vertices, base_level_id, height, family_id)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"The wall path is converted to an OpenPolygonalPath for realization.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Walls with openings: After the wall is created, doors and windows are realized individually. Each opening calls its own realize method that inserts it into the host wall.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"warning: Curved walls\nRevit only supports walls made of lines and arcs. Spline walls are not supported and will throw an error. The commented-out CreateSplineWall API confirms this limitation.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Curtain-Walls","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Curtain Walls","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"curtain_wall(path, bottom_level=level(0), top_level=level(3))","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Created via CreatePathCurtainWall(points, angles, base_level, top_level, family_id, is_structural). The path is decomposed via locs_and_arcs.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Like regular walls, curtain walls do not support spline paths.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Doors","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Doors","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"door(wall_element, location, family=my_door_family)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Doors are inserted into host walls via InsertDoor(delta_from_start, delta_from_ground, host, family_id):","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"delta_from_start: distance along the wall from its start point (X component of the transformed location)\ndelta_from_ground: height above the wall's base level (Y component)\nhost: the wall element reference","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"The door's location is adjusted by the family's location_transform — by default, this shifts by half the door width to convert from Khepri's edge-referenced position to Revit's center-referenced insertion.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"note: Note\nbackend_add_door is not yet implemented (calls finish_this()). Doors must be specified as part of the wall's openings list.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Windows","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Windows","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"window(wall_element, location, family=my_window_family)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Windows are inserted via InsertWindow(delta_from_start, delta_from_ground, host, family_id, param_names, param_values):","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Same positioning as doors (delta from start, delta from ground, host wall)\nAdditionally passes instance parameters from the family's instance_map — this is how window Width and Height are set","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"The default window family uses instance_map with explicit to_feet conversion:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"\"Width\"  => f -> f.width * to_feet\n\"Height\" => f -> f.height * to_feet","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Windows can be added to existing walls:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"backend_add_window(revit, wall_element, location, window_family)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Structural-Elements","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Structural Elements","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/#Columns","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Columns","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Columns come in two variants:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"# Level-based column (between two levels)\ncolumn(xy(5, 5), bottom_level=level(0), top_level=level(3))","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Created via CreateColumn(location, base_level_id, top_level_id, family_id).","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"# Free-standing column (between two points)\nfree_column(xyz(5, 5, 0), 3.0)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Created via CreateColumnPoints(bottom_point, top_point, bottom_level, top_level, family_id). Levels are automatically found or created at the Z elevations of the bottom and top points.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"The default column family is a metric concrete rectangular column with \"b\" (width) and \"h\" (height) parameter mappings.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Beams","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Beams","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"beam(point, height, angle=0)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Created via CreateBeam(p0, p1, rotation_angle, family_id) where p0 is the start point and p1 = p0 + (0, 0, height). Beams are aligned along the top axis.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"The default beam family is metric timber with \"b\" (width) and \"d\" (depth) parameter mappings.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Trusses","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Trusses","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Truss elements use two types:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"TrussBar: Created as a beam via CreateBeam(p0, p1, angle, family_id)\nTrussNode: Created as a small beam stub via CreateBeam(p, p + (0.1, 0, 0), 0, family_id)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Both default to the metric steel W-Wide Flange family.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Circulation-Elements","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Circulation Elements","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/#Railings","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Railings","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Railings support three path types:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"# Line railing (open path)\nrailing(open_polygonal_path([xy(0,0), xy(5,0), xy(5,3)]),\n        level=my_level)\n\n# Polygon railing (closed path)\nrailing(closed_polygonal_path([xy(0,0), xy(5,0), xy(5,3), xy(0,3)]),\n        level=my_level)\n\n# Host-attached railing (attached to a slab, ramp, or stair)\nrailing(some_path, host=my_slab)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Open polygonal → CreateLineRailing(vertices, level_id, family_id)\nClosed polygonal → CreatePolygonRailing(vertices, level_id, family_id)\nHost-attached → InsertRailing(host_ref, family_id)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Ramps","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Ramps","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"ramp(path, bottom_level=level(0), top_level=level(1))","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Created via CreateRamp(p0, p1, width, thickness, base_level_id, base_offset, top_offset). The path's start and end points define the ramp geometry; width and thickness come from the family.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Stairs","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Stairs","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Straight stairs:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"stair(base_point, direction, bottom_level=level(0), top_level=level(3))","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Created via CreateStraightStair(base_point, direction, width, base_level_id, top_level_id, family_id).","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Spiral stairs:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"spiral_stair(center, radius, start_angle, included_angle,\n             clockwise=false, bottom_level=level(0), top_level=level(3))","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Created via CreateSpiralStair(center, radius, start_angle, included_angle, clockwise, width, base_level_id, top_level_id, family_id).","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Stair-Landings","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Stair Landings","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Stair landings are delegated to b_slab — they are created as floor slabs at the specified level.","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/#Fixtures","page":"BIM Elements","title":"Fixtures","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Fixtures (toilets, closets, sinks) are placed on host elements (typically slabs) with a location and direction:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"# All use CreateElementLocDirOnHost(location, direction, host, family_id)\ntoilet(location, host=my_slab)\ncloset(location, host=my_slab)\nsink(location, host=my_slab)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Each fixture family has a location_transform that adjusts the position and rotation:","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"Fixture Transform\nToilet Rotate 90° via loc_from_o_phi(c, π/2), shift Y by -0.12\nCloset No transform\nSink Rotate 90° via loc_from_o_phi(c, π/2)","category":"page"},{"location":"elements/","page":"BIM Elements","title":"BIM Elements","text":"The direction vector is always vx(1, c.cs) in the fixture's coordinate system.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#families","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The family system is the most important part of using KhepriRevit effectively. Revit organizes all building elements into families — parametric templates that define an element's geometry, behavior, and available parameters. Khepri bridges this concept through set_backend_family, which maps Khepri's backend-agnostic families to Revit-specific family configurations.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Family-Types-Overview","page":"Families","title":"Family Types Overview","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Revit has three family types, each represented by a Khepri struct:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Type Struct Use Case Revit Concept\nSystem Family RevitSystemFamily Built-in types: walls, slabs, roofs, floors, ceilings, pipes Types defined by Revit itself — not loadable from files\nFile Family RevitFileFamily Loadable .rfa files: columns, beams, windows, doors, furniture, fixtures Loadable families — .rfa files from Revit's library or custom-created\nIn-Place Family RevitInPlaceFamily Unique project-specific elements Reserved for future use — not yet implemented","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#RevitSystemFamily","page":"Families","title":"RevitSystemFamily","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"revit_system_family(family_map=(), instance_map=(), location_transform=(f, p)->p)","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"System families are Revit's built-in types. They cannot be loaded from external files — they are part of the Revit project template. Walls, slabs, roofs, ceilings, and railings are all system families.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Parameters","page":"Families","title":"Parameters","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"family_map: Pairs of \"RevitParamName\" => f -> expression that extract values from the Khepri family f to set Revit family type parameters. These define the type variant (e.g., wall thickness).\ninstance_map: Same format, but for instance parameters — values set per-placement rather than per-type.\nlocation_transform: A function (family, position) -> adjusted_position that adjusts the placement coordinates. Used when Revit's insertion point convention differs from Khepri's (e.g., doors insert at center but Khepri specifies the edge).","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#How-It-Works","page":"Families","title":"How It Works","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"When a shape using a system family is realized:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"family_ref(b, family) calls backend_get_family_ref(b, family, rvtf)\nIf the family type hasn't been resolved yet, the family_map parameters are collected and sent to Revit via FamilyElement(0, param_names, param_values) — the 0 signals a system family lookup\nRevit returns an ElementId for the resolved family type\nThe result is cached in the parent Khepri family's ref::IdDict{Backend, Any} slot (generated by @deffamily) so subsequent calls reuse it","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Example","page":"Families","title":"Example","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The default door family shifts the insertion point by half the door width, because Revit expects doors positioned at their center:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"set_backend_family(\n  default_door_family(),\n  revit,\n  revit_system_family(\n    [],   # no family_map\n    [],   # no instance_map\n    (f, p) -> p + vx(f.width/2, p.cs)))  # shift by half width","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#RevitFileFamily","page":"Families","title":"RevitFileFamily","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"revit_file_family(path, family_map=(), instance_map=(), location_transform=(f, p)->p)","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"File families are loaded from .rfa files — Revit's loadable family format. Columns, beams, windows, doors, furniture, and plumbing fixtures typically use file families.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Parameters-2","page":"Families","title":"Parameters","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"path: Absolute path to the .rfa file. Use revit_library_path to resolve paths relative to Revit's installed library.\nfamily_map: Maps Revit type parameter names to functions that extract values from the Khepri family. These define the parametric dimensions of the family type (e.g., column width \"b\" and height \"h\").\ninstance_map: Maps Revit instance parameter names. Used when parameters are set per-placement (e.g., window \"Width\" and \"Height\").\nlocation_transform: Adjusts placement coordinates, same as for system families.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Resolution-Pipeline","page":"Families","title":"Resolution Pipeline","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"When a shape using a file family is realized:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"family_ref(b, family) calls backend_get_family_ref(b, family, rvtf)\nThe .rfa file is loaded via LoadFamily(rvtf.path) — Revit returns the family's ElementId\nA specific family type is created via FamilyElement(family_ref, param_names, param_values) using the family_map parameter mappings\nRevit creates (or finds) a type with those parameter values and returns its ElementId","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"note: Note\nFamily files are cached by their full normalized path inside the C# plugin (pathToFamily in Primitives.cs). The cache survives across multiple realizations within a single Revit session. Edit a .rfa mid-session and you must restart Revit for the change to take effect — the cache is not mtime-aware.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Example-2","page":"Families","title":"Example","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The default column family loads a concrete rectangular column and maps Khepri's profile dimensions to Revit's \"b\" (width) and \"h\" (height) parameters:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"set_backend_family(\n  default_column_family(),\n  revit,\n  revit_file_family(\n    revit_library_path(\"Metric Library\",\n      raw\"Structural Columns\\Concrete\\M_Concrete-Rectangular-Column.rfa\"),\n    [\"b\" => f -> f.profile.dx, \"h\" => f -> f.profile.dy]))","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Library-Path-Resolution","page":"Families","title":"Library Path Resolution","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"revit_library_path(root, path)","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Resolves a path relative to Revit's installed content library. The root argument specifies which library branch to use.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Arguments","page":"Families","title":"Arguments","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"root: The library root — typically \"Metric Library\" or \"Imperial Library\".\npath: The relative path within that library to the .rfa file.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Usage","page":"Families","title":"Usage","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"# Metric column\nrevit_library_path(\"Metric Library\",\n  raw\"Structural Columns\\Concrete\\M_Concrete-Rectangular-Column.rfa\")\n\n# Metric window\nrevit_library_path(\"Metric Library\",\n  raw\"Windows\\M_Instance-Window-Fixed.rfa\")\n\n# Imperial beam\nrevit_library_path(\"Imperial Library\",\n  raw\"Structural Framing\\Wood\\Lumber.rfa\")","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"warning: Use raw strings for Windows paths\nAlways use raw\"...\" for the path argument to avoid backslash escaping issues. Without raw, \\M or \\S would be interpreted as escape sequences.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The function calls InstalledLibraryPath(root) on the connected Revit instance to get the actual library base path, then joins it with the relative path. This means paths adapt automatically to different Revit installations and versions.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#default-families","page":"Families","title":"Default Family Mappings","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"These mappings are registered automatically in after_connecting when the Revit backend is activated. They define how standard Khepri families map to Revit families.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#System-Families-(No-.rfa-File)","page":"Families","title":"System Families (No .rfa File)","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Khepri Family Notes\ndefault_wall_family() Default wall type from the project template\ndefault_curtain_wall_family() Default curtain wall type\ndefault_slab_family() Default floor type\ndefault_panel_family() Default panel type\ndefault_ceiling_family() Default ceiling type\ndefault_railing_family() Default railing type\ndefault_ramp_family() Default ramp type\ndefault_stair_family() Default stair type\ndefault_stair_landing_family() Default stair landing type\ndefault_door_family() Default door; instance_map: \"Width\" => f->to_revit(f.width), \"Height\" => f->to_revit(f.height); location_transform: shift by f.width/2. Routes through the InsertDoorWithParams RPC.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#File-Families-(Loadable-.rfa)","page":"Families","title":"File Families (Loadable .rfa)","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Khepri Family .rfa Path Parameter Mapping\ndefault_window_family() Windows\\M_Instance-Window-Fixed.rfa instance_map: \"Width\" => f->to_revit(f.width), \"Height\" => f->to_revit(f.height); location_transform: shift by f.width/2\ndefault_column_family() Structural Columns\\Concrete\\M_Concrete-Rectangular-Column.rfa family_map: \"b\" => f->f.profile.dx, \"h\" => f->f.profile.dy\ndefault_beam_family() Structural Framing\\Wood\\M_Timber.rfa family_map: \"b\" => f->f.profile.dx, \"d\" => f->f.profile.dy\ndefault_truss_bar_family() Structural Framing\\Steel\\M_W-Wide Flange.rfa none\ndefault_truss_node_family() Structural Framing\\Steel\\M_W-Wide Flange.rfa none\ndefault_toilet_family() Plumbing\\Architectural\\Fixtures\\Water Closets\\M_Toilet-Domestic-3D.rfa location_transform: rotate 90° + shift Y by -0.12\ndefault_closet_family() Furniture\\Storage\\M_Shelving.rfa none\ndefault_sink_family() Plumbing\\Architectural\\Fixtures\\Sinks\\M_Sink Vanity-Square.rfa location_transform: rotate 90°","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"All file family paths are relative to the \"Metric Library\" root and are resolved via revit_library_path.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Unit-Conversion:-to_feet-and-to_revit","page":"Families","title":"Unit Conversion: to_feet and to_revit","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The constant to_feet ≈ 3.28084 converts meters to feet. The helper to_revit(x) = x * to_feet is exported for use inside instance_map lambdas where the conversion must happen explicitly.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The key distinction:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"family_map parameters flow through the C# RPC's Length parameter, which rLength() automatically multiplies by to_feet. Pass meters; the wire takes care of conversion. Do not call to_revit here.\ninstance_map parameters flow through the C# RPC's object[] values, which is decoded as raw doubles with no conversion. The Julia caller must pre-convert via to_revit(...).","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"# Column family_map: values in meters (no conversion — rLength handles it)\n\"b\" => f -> f.profile.dx\n\"h\" => f -> f.profile.dy\n\n# Window instance_map: values in feet (explicit conversion)\n\"Width\"  => f -> to_revit(f.width)\n\"Height\" => f -> to_revit(f.height)","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"tip: Tip\nIf a family parameter produces elements with incorrect dimensions, check whether the Revit parameter is shown in the Properties panel in display units (mm/m → use to_revit) or as a built-in Length (no conversion).","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#multi-step-parameter-pattern","page":"Families","title":"Multi-step Parameter Pattern","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Some Revit families need their parameters set in two phases:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Type-level parameters (the family_map) are baked into a duplicated FamilySymbol before the family instance is created. Two placements that request the same type-level values share a single duplicated symbol — i.e. they are the same Revit type. Use this for parameters that define the variant: profile dimensions, glazing pattern, structural material.\nInstance-level parameters (the instance_map) are applied per-placement after NewFamilyInstance via the C# SetParameters helper. Each placement gets its own value. Use this for parameters that vary per-occurrence: opening angle, sill height, mark, comments.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The canonical example is a circular window where the radius defines the type (two windows with the same radius can share a symbol) but the opening angle is per-instance (each window can be open by a different amount):","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"round_win = window_family(width=1.2, height=1.2)\nset_backend_family(round_win, revit, revit_file_family(\n  raw\"C:\\MyFamilies\\ParametricRoundWindow.rfa\",\n  [\"Radius\"        => f -> to_revit(f.width/2)],   # type-level, baked\n  [\"Opening Angle\" => f -> π/4]))                   # instance-level, per-placement","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The revit_casement_window_family helper provides the same pattern with sensible defaults for the Metric library casement window:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"casement = window_family(width=1.2, height=1.4)\nset_backend_family(casement, revit, revit_casement_window_family(\n  revit_library_path(\"Metric Library\", raw\"Windows\\M_Casement-Double.rfa\"),\n  width=f -> to_revit(f.width),     # type-level Width\n  sill =f -> to_revit(0.95)))       # instance-level Default Sill Height","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"When designing your own split:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"A parameter is a good fit for family_map when changing it should produce a new logical type (a 600 mm column is \"different\" from a 700 mm column).\nA parameter is a good fit for instance_map when two adjacent instances legitimately have different values (one window open, the next one shut).","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Customization-Examples","page":"Families","title":"Customization Examples","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/#Changing-the-Default-Column-to-a-Steel-I-Beam","page":"Families","title":"Changing the Default Column to a Steel I-Beam","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"set_backend_family(\n  default_column_family(),\n  revit,\n  revit_file_family(\n    revit_library_path(\"Metric Library\",\n      raw\"Structural Columns\\Steel\\M_W-Wide Flange-Column.rfa\"),\n    [\"bf\" => f -> f.profile.dx, \"d\" => f -> f.profile.dy]))","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Here \"bf\" (flange width) and \"d\" (depth) are the Revit parameter names for the W-shape family. You need to check the .rfa file's parameter names in Revit's Family Editor to find the correct names.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Using-a-Custom-.rfa-File-for-Windows","page":"Families","title":"Using a Custom .rfa File for Windows","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"set_backend_family(\n  default_window_family(),\n  revit,\n  revit_file_family(\n    raw\"C:\\MyFamilies\\Custom-Casement-Window.rfa\",\n    [],  # no family_map (type params)\n    [\"Width\" => f -> f.width * to_feet, \"Height\" => f -> f.height * to_feet],\n    (f, p) -> p + vx(f.width/2, p.cs)))","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Custom .rfa files use an absolute path instead of revit_library_path.","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Mapping-Additional-Parameters","page":"Families","title":"Mapping Additional Parameters","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"To add flange width mapping to a beam family:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"set_backend_family(\n  default_beam_family(),\n  revit,\n  revit_file_family(\n    revit_library_path(\"Metric Library\",\n      raw\"Structural Framing\\Steel\\M_W-Wide Flange.rfa\"),\n    [\"bf\" => f -> f.profile.dx,    # flange width\n     \"d\"  => f -> f.profile.dy,    # depth\n     \"tf\" => f -> 0.015,           # flange thickness (fixed)\n     \"tw\" => f -> 0.010]))         # web thickness (fixed)","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Creating-a-Per-Element-Family-Override","page":"Families","title":"Creating a Per-Element Family Override","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"You can override the family for individual elements:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"# Define a specific column family\nsteel_col = column_family(profile=rectangular_profile(0.3, 0.3))\nset_backend_family(steel_col, revit, revit_file_family(\n  revit_library_path(\"Metric Library\",\n    raw\"Structural Columns\\Steel\\M_W-Wide Flange-Column.rfa\"),\n  [\"bf\" => f -> f.profile.dx, \"d\" => f -> f.profile.dy]))\n\n# Use it for specific columns\ncolumn(xy(0, 0), family=steel_col)\ncolumn(xy(5, 0), family=steel_col)\n\n# Other columns still use the default\ncolumn(xy(10, 0))  # uses default_column_family()","category":"page"},{"location":"families/#Finding-Revit-Parameter-Names","page":"Families","title":"Finding Revit Parameter Names","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"To customize family mappings, you need to know the Revit parameter names for your .rfa file. There are several ways to find them:","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"Revit Family Editor: Open the .rfa file → Family Types dialog → lists all parameters with their names\nRevit Properties Panel: Place an instance → select it → the Properties panel shows instance and type parameters\nRevit API: Use Element.Parameters to enumerate programmatically","category":"page"},{"location":"families/","page":"Families","title":"Families","text":"The parameter names used in family_map and instance_map must match exactly (case-sensitive) the names shown in Revit.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#geometry","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Beyond BIM elements, KhepriRevit supports pure geometry primitives, boolean operations, views, rendering, and IFC interoperability.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Geometry-Primitives","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry Primitives","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"KhepriRevit implements all standard Khepri geometry operations:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Solid-Primitives","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Solid Primitives","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"# Box\nbox(corner_point, dx, dy, dz)\n\n# Sphere\nsphere(center, radius)\n\n# Cylinder\ncylinder(bottom_center, radius, height)\n\n# Cone\ncone(bottom_center, radius, height)\n\n# Cone frustum (truncated cone)\ncone_frustum(bottom_center, bottom_radius, height, top_radius)\n\n# Pyramid frustum\npyramid_frustum(bottom_vertices, top_vertices)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"All primitives use the local coordinate system for orientation. For example, a cylinder's axis is vz(1, cb.cs) — the Z-axis of the bottom center's coordinate system.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Extruded-Contours","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Extruded Contours","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"extrude(path, direction)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Extruded contours support:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Smooth and faceted contour outlines\nHoles (inner paths)\nArbitrary extrusion vectors","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"The implementation uses ExtrudedContour(contour_vertices, smooth_contour, hole_vertices, smooth_holes, direction_vector).","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Surface-Grids","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Surface Grids","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"surface_grid(points_matrix, closed_u, closed_v)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Creates a surface from a grid of points via SurfaceFromGrid(m, n, points, closed_m, closed_n, level).","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Boolean-Operations","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Boolean Operations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"KhepriRevit declares HasBooleanOps{true}, which enables KhepriBase's implicit boolean operation path. This means standard Khepri boolean operations work:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"# Union\nunion(shape1, shape2)\n\n# Intersection\nintersection(shape1, shape2)\n\n# Subtraction\nsubtraction(shape1, shape2)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"The Revit plugin provides Union, Intersection, and Subtraction operations that work on ElementId pairs.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"note: Note\nThe direct unite_ref/intersect_ref/subtract_ref methods are currently commented out in the source. Boolean operations work through KhepriBase's implicit boolean path via HasBooleanOps{true}.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Views-and-Rendering","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Views and Rendering","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/#Setting-the-View","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Setting the View","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"set_view(camera_position, target_position, lens=50)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Realized via SetView(camera, target, width, height, lens) where width and height come from render_width() and render_height().","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Getting-the-Current-View","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Getting the Current View","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"get_view()","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Returns (camera, target, lens) by querying GetCamera(), GetTarget(), and GetLens() from Revit.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Rendering","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Rendering","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"render_view(\"output_path\")","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Saves a rendered view to the specified path via RenderView(path).","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Additional-View-Operations","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Additional View Operations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"# Zoom to fit all elements\nzoom_extents(revit)\n\n# Switch to top view\nview_top(revit)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#OBJ-Export-from-Family-Files","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"OBJ Export from Family Files","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"KhepriRevit can extract the graphical representation (mesh geometry and materials) from Revit family files (.rfa) and save them as standard Wavefront OBJ files with accompanying MTL material libraries.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"This is useful for:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Previewing family geometry in external tools (Blender, MeshLab, etc.)\nUsing Revit family meshes in non-Revit rendering pipelines\nArchiving family geometry in an open, portable format","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Exporting-a-Single-Family","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Exporting a Single Family","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"export_family_to_obj(family_path, obj_path)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Opens the .rfa file, extracts all mesh geometry at Fine detail level, and writes an OBJ file (with an accompanying .mtl file in the same directory).","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"family_path: Absolute path to the .rfa file. Use revit_library_path to resolve library-relative paths.\nobj_path: Output path for the .obj file. The .mtl file is created alongside it with the same base name.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Vertex coordinates are converted from Revit's internal units (feet) to meters. Each face in the OBJ is assigned a material group (usemtl) corresponding to the Revit material applied to that face. The MTL file contains diffuse color (Kd) and transparency (d) properties for each material.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"# Export a sink family from the Metric Library\nexport_family_to_obj(\n  revit_library_path(\"Metric Library\",\n    raw\"Plumbing\\Architectural\\Fixtures\\Sinks\\M_Sink Vanity-Square.rfa\"),\n  raw\"C:\\Export\\sink.obj\")\n# Produces: C:\\Export\\sink.obj and C:\\Export\\sink.mtl\n\n# Export a custom family file\nexport_family_to_obj(\n  raw\"C:\\MyFamilies\\Custom-Column.rfa\",\n  raw\"C:\\Export\\custom_column.obj\")","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Exporting-All-Instantiated-Families-from-a-Model","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Exporting All Instantiated Families from a Model","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"export_all_families_to_obj(folder_path)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Iterates over all family instances in the currently loaded Revit document, identifies the unique families that are actually used, and exports each one as an OBJ + MTL pair into the specified folder.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"folder_path: Path to the output directory. Created automatically if it does not exist.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"The output files are named after the Revit family name (with spaces and path-separator characters replaced by underscores). Each unique family is exported only once, regardless of how many instances exist in the model.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"note: Note\nOnly loadable families (those backed by .rfa files) that appear as FamilyInstance elements are exported. System families (walls, floors, roofs, ceilings) are not included, as they do not have standalone .rfa geometry definitions.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"# Load a Revit model\nload_rvt_file(raw\"C:\\Projects\\Building.rvt\")\n\n# Export all instantiated families to a folder\nexport_all_families_to_obj(raw\"C:\\Export\\families\")\n# Produces files like:\n#   C:\\Export\\families\\M_Concrete-Rectangular-Column.obj\n#   C:\\Export\\families\\M_Concrete-Rectangular-Column.mtl\n#   C:\\Export\\families\\M_Instance-Window-Fixed.obj\n#   C:\\Export\\families\\M_Instance-Window-Fixed.mtl\n#   C:\\Export\\families\\M_Sink_Vanity-Square.obj\n#   C:\\Export\\families\\M_Sink_Vanity-Square.mtl\n#   ...","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#OBJ-Format-Details","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"OBJ Format Details","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"The exported files follow the standard Wavefront OBJ/MTL specification:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Vertices (v): Coordinates in meters (converted from Revit's internal feet).\nNormals (vn): Per-triangle face normals, computed from cross products.\nFaces (f): Triangulated; each face references vertex and normal indices in the format v//vn.\nMaterial groups (usemtl): Faces are grouped by their Revit material. Faces with no assigned material use a default material (light gray).\nMTL properties: Diffuse color (Kd) from the Revit material's Color, and dissolve/transparency (d) from the material's Transparency property. Illumination model is set to illum 2 (diffuse + specular).","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#IFC-Interoperability","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"IFC Interoperability","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"KhepriRevit provides three functions for IFC workflow:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Converting-IFC-to-RVT","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Converting IFC to RVT","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"convert_ifc_file(path)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Converts an IFC file to Revit's .rvt format using Revit's built-in IFC importer.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Loading-an-RVT-File","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Loading an RVT File","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"load_rvt_file(path)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Opens a .rvt file in the current Revit session.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Convert-and-Load","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Convert and Load","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"convert_and_load_ifc_file(path)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Convenience function that converts an IFC file to .rvt and then loads the result. The .rvt file is created in the same directory as the IFC file with the same base name.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Querying-Existing-Documents","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Querying Existing Documents","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"KhepriRevit can read elements from existing Revit documents:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"# Get all levels\nlevels = all_levels(revit)\n\n# Get all walls (including doors and windows)\nwalls = all_walls(revit)\n\n# Get walls at a specific level\nwalls_at_level = all_walls_at_level(my_level, revit)\n\n# Get all elements\nelements = all_elements(revit)\n\n# Get all floors, columns, beams, ceilings\nfloors = all_floors(revit)\ncolumns = all_columns(revit)\nbeams = all_beams(revit)\nceilings = all_ceilings(revit)\n\n# Get all doors and windows (as HostedElementInfo with host wall ID)\ndoors = all_doors(revit)\nwindows = all_windows(revit)","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"For full model introspection and code generation from an existing Revit document, see Code Generation.","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Wall-Reconstruction","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Wall Reconstruction","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"all_walls reconstructs full Khepri Wall objects from Revit data:","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Queries wall geometry — supports three path types:\nLine walls via LineWallVertices → OpenPolygonalPath\nArc walls via ArcWallCenter/ArcWallRadius/ArcWallAngles → ArcPath (center, radius, start/end angles in local coordinate system)\nCurtain walls are detected via WallIsCurtainWall and created as CurtainWall shapes\nQueries the bottom level via ElementLevel → creates a Khepri Level\nQueries the top level via WallTopLevel:\nIf valid → creates a connected Level\nIf -1 (void) → creates an unconnected level using the wall height from WallHeight","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Element-Selection","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Element Selection","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"# Highlight an element in Revit\nhighlight_element(element_id)\n\n# Get currently selected elements\nselected = get_selected_elements()","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Deleting-Elements","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Deleting Elements","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"delete_all_shapes()","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Removes all elements from the Revit document via DeleteAllElements().","category":"page"},{"location":"geometry/#Known-Limitations","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Known Limitations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"geometry/","page":"Geometry & Interop","title":"Geometry & Interop","text":"Materials are not supported: The Revit backend does not use Khepri's material system. The material_ref function is commented out. Elements use whatever material is defined in their Revit family type.\nSpline paths are not supported: Walls, curtain walls, slabs, and roofs can only use paths composed of lines and arcs. Spline paths throw an error.\nDoors and windows: Doors and windows can be added to walls as part of the wall's openings (via wall_with_openings). The loc parameter uses wall-relative coordinates: loc.x is the distance along the wall, loc.y is the sill height.\nSome family parameters require to_feet conversion: Whether a parameter needs conversion depends on how the specific Revit family defines its parameters. See the Families page.\nNamed geometry variants: Some geometry operations have Named variants (e.g., ConeFrustumNamed, PyramidFrustumNamed, ExtrudedContourNamed) that accept a name parameter and material ID. These are available through the remote API but not all are exposed through standard Khepri operations.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#codegen","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"KhepriRevit can introspect an existing Revit model and generate a portable Khepri Julia script that recreates it. This is useful for capturing existing Revit designs as parametric Khepri programs.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Quick-Start","page":"Code Generation","title":"Quick Start","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"using KhepriRevit\n\n# Connect to Revit (must be running with the Khepri plugin loaded)\n# Then generate code from the current document:\ngenerate_khepri_code(\"my_building.jl\")","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"This produces a .jl file containing levels, families, backend mappings, element calls (walls, floors, columns, beams, ceilings), and model groups that reproduce the Revit model.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Sample-Output","page":"Code Generation","title":"Sample Output","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"# Auto-generated Khepri code from Revit model\n# Generated on: 2026-03-08T14:30:00\nusing KhepriRevit\n\nlevel_0 = level(0.0)\nlevel_1 = level(3.5)\n\nwall_basic_wall_generic_200mm = wall_family()\nset_backend_family(wall_basic_wall_generic_200mm, revit, revit_system_family())\n\ncolumn_m_round_column_450mm = column_family()\nset_backend_family(column_m_round_column_450mm, revit,\n  revit_file_family(raw\"C:\\ProgramData\\...\\M_Round Column.rfa\"))\n\nwall(xy(0.0, 0.0), xy(10.0, 0.0),\n  level=level_0, top_level=level_1,\n  family=wall_basic_wall_generic_200mm)\n\nfor x in 0.0:5.0:10.0\n  for y in 0.0:5.0:10.0\n    column(xyz(x, y, 0.0), level=level_0, top_level=level_1,\n      family=column_m_round_column_450mm)\n  end\nend","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Pipeline-Architecture","page":"Code Generation","title":"Pipeline Architecture","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"The pipeline has five phases, each a pure function:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Introspect (introspect_model) — queries Revit for all elements and their family metadata, returns a NamedTuple of Khepri shapes\nMeta-program (model_to_expr) — converts each shape to a Julia Expr AST node via meta_program\nTransform — a sequence of Expr → Expr passes that clean up and compress the raw AST\nPrint (expr_to_string) — pretty-prints the final Expr to Julia source code with section-aware formatting\nWrite — saves the string to a file","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"generate_khepri_code orchestrates all phases:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"function generate_khepri_code(output_path; b=revit)\n  model = introspect_model(b=b)\n  raw_expr = model_to_expr(model)\n  passes = [extract_levels,\n            extract_families,\n            add_backend_families(model),\n            loop_rerolling,\n            detect_level_repetition,\n            add_header]\n  refined_expr = foldl((e, pass) -> pass(e), passes, init=raw_expr)\n  code = expr_to_string(refined_expr)\n  write(output_path, code)\nend","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"The pipeline design was inspired by the multi-pass code generation approach described in Tomás Grelha da Cunha's thesis on Khepri model introspection.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Using-Individual-Pipeline-Stages","page":"Code Generation","title":"Using Individual Pipeline Stages","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Each stage can be called independently for inspection or debugging.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Phase-1:-Introspection","page":"Code Generation","title":"Phase 1: Introspection","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"model = introspect_model(b=revit)","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Returns a NamedTuple with fields:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Field Element type Revit queries used\nlevels Level DocLevels\nwalls Wall DocWalls, DocDoors, DocWindows\nfloors Slab DocFloors, FloorBoundaryVertices\ncolumns Column DocColumns, ColumnLocation\nbeams Beam DocBeams, BeamLocation\nceilings Ceiling DocCeilings, CeilingBoundaryVertices\ngroups NamedTuple DocGroups, GroupMemberIds, GroupLocation","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Walls include any hosted doors and windows, attached as w.doors and w.windows. Family metadata (name, type, system vs. file, path) is collected per element and stored in a side-channel for use by later passes.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Elements that belong to Revit model groups are excluded from the individual element queries (walls, floors, etc.) and instead introspected as part of their group. The groups field contains a list of group types, each with its member shapes and instance locations.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Phase-2:-Model-to-Expr","page":"Code Generation","title":"Phase 2: Model to Expr","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"raw_expr = model_to_expr(model)","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Iterates over all shapes in the model and calls meta_program on each, producing Julia AST nodes. For example, a wall becomes:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":":(wall(xy(0.0, 0.0), xy(10.0, 0.0), level=level(0.0), top_level=level(3.5),\n       family=wall_family()))","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Walls with doors or windows produce wall_with_openings(...) calls instead. Family expressions are registered in a side-channel (_family_expr_meta) so that later passes can map them to Revit-specific families.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Phase-3:-Transform-Passes","page":"Code Generation","title":"Phase 3: Transform Passes","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Each pass is an Expr → Expr function applied in sequence via foldl:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"extract_levels — finds all level(h) calls, deduplicates by height, and replaces them with named variables (level_0, level_1, ...) sorted by height.\nextract_families — finds all *_family() calls, deduplicates them, and extracts them into named variables using Revit metadata for descriptive names (e.g., wall_basic_wall_generic_200mm).\nadd_backend_families(model) — inserts set_backend_family calls after each family assignment, mapping the Khepri family to its Revit-specific counterpart (revit_system_family() or revit_file_family(path)).\nloop_rerolling — detects repeated shape calls that form 1D or 2D grid patterns and compresses them into for loops with range expressions.\ndetect_level_repetition — (planned) detects identical floor plans repeated across levels and compresses them into a level loop. Currently a no-op.\nadd_header — prepends a using KhepriRevit statement and date comments.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Phase-4:-Pretty-Printing","page":"Code Generation","title":"Phase 4: Pretty-Printing","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"code = expr_to_string(refined_expr)","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Produces formatted Julia source code with:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"2-space indentation\n80-character line wrapping (long calls break across lines)\nBlank lines between sections (header, levels, families, elements)","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Transform-Passes-in-Detail","page":"Code Generation","title":"Transform Passes in Detail","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/#Loop-Rerolling","page":"Code Generation","title":"Loop Rerolling","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"The loop rerolling pass detects sequences of 4+ structurally similar shape calls where only leaf values (numbers, coordinates) vary, and converts them into for loops.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"1D example — 5 columns along a line:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Before:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"column(xyz(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(3.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(6.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(9.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(12.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"After:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"for x in 0.0:3.0:12.0\n  column(xyz(x, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\nend","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"2D example — 9 columns in a 3×3 grid:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Before:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"column(xyz(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(0.0, 5.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(0.0, 10.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(5.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(5.0, 5.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(5.0, 10.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(10.0, 0.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(10.0, 5.0, 0.0), ...)\ncolumn(xyz(10.0, 10.0, 0.0), ...)","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"After:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"for x in 0.0:5.0:10.0\n  for y in 0.0:5.0:10.0\n    column(xyz(x, y, 0.0), ...)\n  end\nend","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"The algorithm:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Groups consecutive calls with the same function name and argument count\nUses expr_diff to find which AST positions vary between calls\nFor 1 varying position → tries 1D range; for 2 → tries 2D nested grid\nValues must form an arithmetic progression (uniform spacing) to become a range expression; otherwise they become an explicit vector [v1, v2, ...]","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Level-and-Family-Extraction","page":"Code Generation","title":"Level and Family Extraction","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Level extraction finds every level(height) expression in the AST, collects unique heights, sorts them, and assigns variables level_0, level_1, etc. All occurrences are then replaced by the variable name.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Family extraction does the same for *_family() calls. Variable names are derived from Revit metadata collected during introspection:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"category_familyName_typeName","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"For example, wall_family() from a \"Basic Wall : Generic - 200mm\" element becomes wall_basic_wall_generic_200mm.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Backend family mapping inserts a set_backend_family call immediately after each family assignment. System families (built into Revit) use revit_system_family(); file-based families use revit_file_family(raw\"path\").","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Generated-Code-Structure","page":"Code Generation","title":"Generated Code Structure","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"The output file has five sections, separated by blank lines:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Header — comment lines and using KhepriRevit\nLevels — level_N = level(height) assignments, sorted by height\nFamilies — fam_var = *_family() assignments, each followed by its set_backend_family call\nElements — shape calls (wall, slab, column, beam, ceiling, wall_with_openings) and for loops from rerolling\nGroups — factory function definitions, group() assignments, and group_instance() placements","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#AST-Utilities","page":"Code Generation","title":"AST Utilities","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"The pipeline provides general-purpose Expr manipulation functions:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"map_expr(f, e) — bottom-up map over an Expr tree; applies f to every node after recursing into children\ncollect_exprs(pred, e) — collect all sub-expressions matching a predicate\nexpr_diff(e1, e2) — structural comparison returning a list of (path, val1, val2) triples where path is a vector of arg indices\nexpr_replace_at(e, path, val) — replace the value at a given path in an Expr tree","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Extending-the-Pipeline","page":"Code Generation","title":"Extending the Pipeline","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"To add a new transform pass:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Define a function my_pass(e::Expr) :: Expr\nAdd it to the passes list in generate_khepri_code at the desired position","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"To add support for a new element type (e.g., ramps):","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Implement all_ramps(b) to query Revit\nAdd meta_program methods for the new shape type (in KhepriBase or locally)\nAdd the new shapes to introspect_model and model_to_expr\nUpdate extract_families if the element has a family","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Model-Groups","page":"Code Generation","title":"Model Groups","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Revit model groups (repeated collections of elements placed at multiple locations) are mapped to Khepri's group/group_instance abstraction.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#How-Groups-Are-Introspected","page":"Code Generation","title":"How Groups Are Introspected","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"All Revit Group elements are queried via DocGroups\nMember element IDs are collected via GroupMemberIds and excluded from individual element queries (walls, floors, etc.) to avoid duplication\nGroups are deduplicated by GroupTypeId — only one representative instance per group type is introspected for its member shapes\nEach group type records: member shapes (with doors/windows attached to walls), and all instance locations","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Generated-Code-Pattern","page":"Code Generation","title":"Generated Code Pattern","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"For each group type, three constructs are emitted:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"# 1. Factory function: creates member shapes at group-relative coordinates\nfunction group_entrance_factory()\n  wall(open_polygonal_path([xyz(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), xyz(5.0, 0.0, 0.0)]),\n    level=level_0, family=wall_basic_wall_generic_200mm)\n  slab(...)\nend\n\n# 2. Group definition: stores the factory for later instantiation\ngroup_entrance = group(\"group_entrance\", factory=group_entrance_factory)\n\n# 3. Instances: one per Revit group instance location\ngroup_instance(group_entrance, xyz(10.0, 5.0, 0.0))\ngroup_instance(group_entrance, xyz(25.0, 5.0, 0.0))","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Member coordinates are translated to be relative to the first instance location (which becomes the group's origin). Each group_instance call re-creates the member shapes at the specified location via the factory function.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Backend-Realization","page":"Code Generation","title":"Backend Realization","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Revit: Since Revit doesn't expose a programmatic group creation API, Group realize is a no-op and each GroupInstance re-invokes the factory function within a translated coordinate system, creating independent copies of the member shapes at each instance location.\nAutoCAD/Unity (via Block/BlockInstance): Backends with native block/group support can implement realize(b, s::Group) to create a block definition from the member shapes, and realize(b, s::GroupInstance) to instantiate it.","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#The-group/group_instance-Abstraction","page":"Code Generation","title":"The group/group_instance Abstraction","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"Defined in KhepriBase as @defproxy types:","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"@defproxy(group, Shape0D, name::String=\"Group\", shapes::Shapes=Shape[],\n          factory::Union{Function,Nothing}=nothing)\n@defproxy(group_instance, Shape0D, group::Group=required(), loc::Loc=u0())","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"name: descriptive identifier (derived from Revit's GroupType.Name)\nshapes: collected member shapes (may be empty when using factory-based instantiation)\nfactory: zero-argument function that creates member shapes when called; used by backends without native group support to re-create shapes at each instance location\nloc: world-space location for the group instance","category":"page"},{"location":"codegen/#Limitations","page":"Code Generation","title":"Limitations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"codegen/","page":"Code Generation","title":"Code Generation","text":"detect_level_repetition is not yet implemented (returns input unchanged)\nFamily parameters (thickness, width, material) are not extracted from Revit; families are created with default parameters and rely on set_backend_family to map them back to the correct Revit type\nOnly element types with existing *_from_ref helpers are introspected (levels, walls, floors, columns, beams, ceilings, doors, windows, groups)\nCurtain walls are detected but generated as regular wall() calls with a curtain_wall_family()\nRoofs and stairs are not yet introspected","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"CurrentModule = KhepriRevit","category":"page"},{"location":"#KhepriRevit","page":"Home","title":"KhepriRevit","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"KhepriRevit is a Khepri backend for Autodesk Revit, enabling parametric BIM modeling from Julia. It communicates with Revit through a C# plugin over TCP, giving you programmatic access to Revit's full BIM capabilities — walls, slabs, roofs, columns, beams, doors, windows, and more — using the same Khepri code that works with every other backend.","category":"page"},{"location":"#Architecture","page":"Home","title":"Architecture","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"KhepriRevit is a SocketBackend using the :RVT protocol (which extends the :CS binary protocol). Julia acts as the TCP server; the Revit C# plugin connects as a client on port 11001.","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Property Value\nBackend type SocketBackend{RVTKey, Int64}\nReference IDs Int64 — Revit ElementId values\nProtocol :RVT (extends :CS binary)\nDefault port 11001\nCoordinate system Right-handed Z-up (same as Khepri — no transforms needed)\nUnits Khepri uses SI (meters); Revit uses feet internally\nBoolean ops Full CSG support (HasBooleanOps{true})","category":"page"},{"location":"#Unit-Conversion","page":"Home","title":"Unit Conversion","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Khepri works in SI units (meters). Revit's internal unit system uses feet. The constant to_feet ≈ 3.28084 handles conversion where needed. Most plugin operations accept SI values directly, but some family parameters (notably window Width and Height) require explicit conversion — see the Families page for details.","category":"page"},{"location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","title":"Quick Start","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"using KhepriRevit\nusing KhepriBase\n\n# Activate the Revit backend\nbackend(revit)\n\n# Create a level and some BIM elements\nwall(xy(0, 0), xy(10, 0))\nwall(xy(10, 0), xy(10, 8))\nslab(rectangular_path(xy(0, 0), 10, 8))\ncolumn(xy(5, 4))","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"This requires Revit running with the Khepri plugin installed and listening on TCP port 11001. See Setup for installation instructions.","category":"page"},{"location":"#Key-Features","page":"Home","title":"Key Features","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"BIM-native families: Three family types — RevitSystemFamily for built-in types (walls, slabs), RevitFileFamily for loadable .rfa files (columns, windows), and RevitInPlaceFamily (reserved for future use). See Families.\nLevel management: Automatic level creation at specified elevations via FindOrCreateLevelAtElevation.\nBoolean operations: Full CSG support — union, intersection, subtraction.\nIFC interoperability: Import and convert IFC files with convert_ifc_file and load_rvt_file.\nOBJ export: Extract mesh geometry and materials from Revit family files with export_family_to_obj, or batch-export all instantiated families from a loaded model with export_all_families_to_obj.\nBIM elements: Walls, floors, roofs, ceilings, doors, windows, columns, beams, curtain walls, railings, ramps, stairs, and plumbing fixtures. See BIM Elements.\nGeometry primitives: Box, sphere, cone, cylinder, pyramid, extruded contours, surface grids. See Geometry & Interop.\nDocument querying: Read existing Revit models with all_walls, all_floors, all_levels, and all_elements.","category":"page"},{"location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","title":"Documentation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"setup.md\", \"families.md\", \"elements.md\", \"geometry.md\"]\nDepth = 2","category":"page"},{"location":"#API-Reference","page":"Home","title":"API Reference","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Modules = [KhepriRevit]","category":"page"},{"location":"#KhepriRevit.revit_casement_window_family-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","title":"KhepriRevit.revit_casement_window_family","text":"Sugar for the type-vs-instance split: type-level Width baked into the symbol, instance-level sill height set per placement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#KhepriRevit.to_revit-Tuple{Real}","page":"Home","title":"KhepriRevit.to_revit","text":"torevit(x) = x * tofeet — converts a Khepri length (meters) to Revit's internal feet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"setup/#setup","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/#Plugin-Installation","page":"Setup","title":"Plugin Installation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"KhepriRevit communicates with Revit through a C# plugin that must be installed as a Revit add-in. The plugin is distributed as an ApplicationPlugin bundle (KhepriRevit.bundle) containing:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"KhepriRevit.bundle/\n├── PackageContents.xml    # Version and metadata\n└── Contents/\n    ├── KhepriBase.dll     # Shared Khepri communication library\n    ├── KhepriRevit.dll    # Revit-specific plugin\n    └── KhepriRevit.addin  # Revit add-in manifest","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Automatic-Plugin-Management","page":"Setup","title":"Automatic Plugin Management","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"On first connection, KhepriRevit automatically checks and updates the plugin:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"check_plugin() runs before connecting — called automatically by before_connecting\nupdate_plugin() compares the version in the local Plugin/ folder (shipped with the Julia package) against the installed version\nIf the installed version is older (or missing), the plugin files are copied automatically","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"The plugin is installed to the user ApplicationPlugins folder:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"%APPDATA%\\Autodesk\\ApplicationPlugins\\KhepriRevit.bundle\\","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Plugin-Installation-Paths","page":"Setup","title":"Plugin Installation Paths","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"Revit searches for add-ins in several locations:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"Location Path Scope\nUser Addins %APPDATA%\\Autodesk\\Revit\\Addins\\<version>\\ Current user\nUser ApplicationPlugins %APPDATA%\\Autodesk\\ApplicationPlugins\\ Current user\nMachine Addins C:\\ProgramData\\Autodesk\\Revit\\Addins\\<version>\\ All users\nMachine ApplicationPlugins C:\\ProgramData\\Autodesk\\ApplicationPlugins\\ All users\nAutodesk servers C:\\Program Files\\Autodesk\\Revit <version>\\AddIns\\ Built-in","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"KhepriRevit uses the User ApplicationPlugins path by default.","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Upgrading-the-Plugin-(Developers)","page":"Setup","title":"Upgrading the Plugin (Developers)","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"When the C# plugin source is modified and recompiled in Visual Studio, run:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"upgrade_plugin()","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"This:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"Bumps the minor version in PackageContents.xml\nCopies the compiled DLLs from the Visual Studio output directory to the Julia package's Plugin/ folder\nThe next check_plugin() call will propagate the update to Revit's plugin folder","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"The build configuration is controlled by development_phase (default: \"Debug\"). Change to \"Release\" for production builds.","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Connection","page":"Setup","title":"Connection","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/#Starting-Revit","page":"Setup","title":"Starting Revit","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"KhepriRevit provides a convenience function to launch Revit with a template:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"# The default template path\nrevit_template()  # Returns path to KhepriTemplate.rte\n\n# Launch Revit with the template\nstart_revit()","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"The template file (KhepriTemplate.rte) is located in the Plugin/ directory of the KhepriRevit package.","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Connecting","page":"Setup","title":"Connecting","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"The standard Khepri backend activation handles connection:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"using KhepriRevit\nusing KhepriBase\n\nbackend(revit)  # Connects to Revit on port 11001","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"The connection sequence:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"before_connecting → calls check_plugin() to ensure the plugin is current\nTCP connection established on port 11001 (Julia as server, plugin as client)\nafter_connecting → registers all default family mappings","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Port-Configuration","page":"Setup","title":"Port Configuration","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"The default port is 11001 (defined as revit_port in KhepriBase). If you need a different port, create a new backend instance:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"my_revit = RVT(\"Revit\", 12345, revit_api)\nbackend(my_revit)","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Troubleshooting","page":"Setup","title":"Troubleshooting","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/#\"The-Revit-plugin-is-outdated!-Please,-close-Revit.\"","page":"Setup","title":"\"The Revit plugin is outdated! Please, close Revit.\"","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"This error appears when update_plugin() cannot overwrite the installed DLLs because Revit has them locked. The solution:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"Close Revit completely\nWait for the retry mechanism (it retries up to 10 times with 5-second delays)\nOr restart Julia and try backend(revit) again","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Plugin-Not-Loading-in-Revit","page":"Setup","title":"Plugin Not Loading in Revit","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"Verify the bundle exists at %APPDATA%\\Autodesk\\ApplicationPlugins\\KhepriRevit.bundle\\\nCheck that PackageContents.xml and the Contents/ folder with DLLs are present\nLook in Revit's add-in manager for error messages\nEnsure the Revit version matches the plugin's target framework","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Connection-Timeout","page":"Setup","title":"Connection Timeout","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"Ensure Revit is running and the Khepri plugin is loaded (check Revit's External Tools or add-in manager)\nCheck that no firewall is blocking TCP port 11001\nVerify no other application is using port 11001","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/#Debug-vs-Release-Builds","page":"Setup","title":"Debug vs Release Builds","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"The development_phase variable (default \"Debug\") controls which build output directory is used when running upgrade_plugin(). If you're building the plugin in Release mode in Visual Studio, set:","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"development_phase = \"Release\"","category":"page"},{"location":"setup/","page":"Setup","title":"Setup","text":"before calling upgrade_plugin().","category":"page"}]
}
